WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.018 --> 00:00:03.758
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.758 --> 00:00:09.545
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; It's 1988,

00:00:09.545 --> 00:00:12.800
my mom is staring at a roomful of people

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.190
who will decide the future
of my two older sisters.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:22.450
My parents had been told that their
daughters would not be allowed to enroll

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.260
in the local neighborhood
elementary school.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.155
Instead they would be shipped
to a segregated classroom for

00:00:29.155 --> 00:00:32.258
children with disabilities,
two cities away.

00:00:32.258 --> 00:00:34.710
My mom is furious.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.500
She argues that if the children
of our neighbors and

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.340
friends attend local public school
that her daughters should too.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:49.545
The school board members speak at length,
using lots of words about logistics,

00:00:49.545 --> 00:00:51.540
and capacity and cost.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.016
Two words they never use
are my sisters names.

00:00:57.016 --> 00:00:59.276
Sarah and Allison.

00:01:01.576 --> 00:01:06.172
There were a lot of meetings like
this over the years of my childhood.

00:01:06.172 --> 00:01:08.970
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.320
&gt;&gt; Sara and Allison both have severe

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.420
developmental disabilities, and my parents
advocated tirelessly on their behalf.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.060
Working with institutions ranging
from schools to health providers and

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.190
state agencies.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:25.550
And I remember that there
was a clear difference

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.940
between the organizations that
treated our family like whole,

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.740
real, complex human beings,
and those that didn't.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:41.580
Most of us can remember a time
when we felt dehumanized

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.100
by an experience within a system.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.360
Maybe you're thinking of your last
interaction with a customer service

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.060
hotline, or the DMV, yeah.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:54.790
Perhaps you're remembering a tough
navigation of the health care system.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:58.310
Or an employer who made you
feel like a faceless number.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:08.050
I am fascinated by the question of what it
means to treat people like real people.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.650
What it looks like to offer the dignity,
respect and

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.050
compassion that we all
crave in our interactions.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.380
And I'm especially interested in how we,
as leaders,

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.830
can create organizations,
that do just that.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.150
They treat people like real people.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:34.354
I believe it comes down to designing
our systems specifically and

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:37.101
intentionally for empathy.

00:02:37.101 --> 00:02:43.290
So why is it important for us to learn how
to build empathy into our organizations?

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.970
Well, I hope that all of you have
experienced the impact of human

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.460
kindness within a system.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.210
For me I think about
the Nurse last year who long

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:01.930
after midnight on one of the worst
nights of my life, brought coffee and

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.860
snacks to the bedside where I
sat with the sick family member.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:11.200
It turns out that experiences
of compassion like this one

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.009
have extremely real consequences for
our health.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:20.410
Research right here at Stanford has shown
that patients who receive human kindness

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:25.500
and empathy during their treatment
experience faster healing of wounds,

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.880
reduced pain, anxiety and blood pressure,
and shorter hospital stays.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:35.740
But treating people like real people isn't
just important in the health sector.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.720
Humans are evolutionarily wired

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.860
with what you see Berkley researchers
have coined the compassion instinct.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:50.470
We are driven to empathize with
the feelings of others, and to give and

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.020
seek aid.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:57.330
Both engaging in and receiving acts of
compassion results in physiological and

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.570
psychological benefits, and
quite simply make for happier people.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:10.210
So if we are evolutionarily wired to
be compassionate toward one another,

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.120
why is it that so

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:17.059
many of us feel dehumanized by the systems
and organizations in our lives.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:25.120
Well, when I asked people what made them
feel human the most common responses were,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:32.030
I feel like a real person when people
care about my background and history.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:35.960
When they listen to and
know and acknowledge my story.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:43.206
People will tell you I
am not my disability,

00:04:43.206 --> 00:04:47.822
I am not my leukemia,
I am not my SAT score,

00:04:47.822 --> 00:04:53.850
I am not my depression,
I am not my immigration status,

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:59.906
I am not my gender,
I am not this singular interaction.

00:04:59.906 --> 00:05:00.446
With you.

00:05:03.118 --> 00:05:08.438
After all, we all contain multitudes.

00:05:11.098 --> 00:05:16.083
I believe that our organizations
fail when they are not designed and

00:05:16.083 --> 00:05:18.979
led to acknowledge these multitudes.

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:26.000
Empathy is what allows us in our one on
one interactions to treat each other like

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.900
real people, to understand that there is
a full story behind everyone we meet.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:39.010
So what we need for our organizations
is a way to systematize human empathy.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:46.464
To do this, I suggest starting
with three simple rules.

00:05:46.464 --> 00:05:53.700
Listen, contextualize, and cultivate.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:06:00.580
To systematize empathy, an organization
has to start by identifying It's ears.

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:07.370
What mechanisms does the organization
use to listen to its stakeholders?

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:11.550
Organizational listening can't just be
about a feedback box in the corner.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:16.960
We have to give stakeholders a valid
place at the table as respected partners.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.870
An example in practice
right here in the Bay Area.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.510
Juvenile justice systems are working with
the organization, Fresh Lifelines for

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:31.510
Youth, to develop mechanisms
to listen to youth voices.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.140
These partnerships are helping
to reduce recidivism,

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:39.280
and educating the the justice system
on how to better serve its community.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:45.920
The second rule of building
empathetic organizations is

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.540
operating with a holistic appreciation for
context.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:55.570
When I was working with healthcare
organizations in East Africa,

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:58.900
some of the most effective models I saw

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:03.010
were those that addressed issues
intersecting with patient's health.

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:06.930
&gt;From nutrition and
sanitation to education and employment.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.550
These organizations approach
their patients as whole,

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:16.680
multifaceted people and their medical
outcomes were the better for it.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:22.080
Finally, to cultivate empathy.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.180
Organizations must unlock their most
powerful asset, their own employees.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.335
Research shows that stress, burn out, and

00:07:32.335 --> 00:07:38.075
toxic company culture can squash
our compassion instinct, but

00:07:38.075 --> 00:07:43.195
findings suggest that companies that
form a culture of compassion not only

00:07:43.195 --> 00:07:48.185
boost employee happiness and health,
they also improve their bottom lines.

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:53.470
Plus, compassion is
proven to be contagious.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:59.241
So, if we want our clients and
customers to be treated well,

00:07:59.241 --> 00:08:05.934
we have to start by treating our
teammates like real whole people too.

00:08:05.934 --> 00:08:10.929
Back in 1988, my mom told that school
board that if they didn't let her

00:08:10.929 --> 00:08:15.369
daughters into school, she would
sit with them on the front lawn of

00:08:15.369 --> 00:08:19.040
Magnolia Elementary every
single day until they did.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.270
Well, great job, Mom.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:25.610
My parents ultimately won their fight.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:29.610
Sarah and Alison entered
Magnolia Elementary School and

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:32.610
eventually Alison became
our counties first

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:36.730
fully main stream student with
disabilities to graduate from high school.

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:44.869
My mom is very clear about one important
fact from our families journey.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:52.390
It was not until there was organizational
reform in districts in schools.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:56.230
Then my sisters were truly
treated like real people.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:02.092
So it’s time for
all of us to own the fact that

00:09:02.092 --> 00:09:06.509
individual acts of
kindness are not enough.

00:09:06.509 --> 00:09:13.214
It is time for us to take organizational
empathy out of the realm of the random,

00:09:13.214 --> 00:09:19.421
out of the realm of above and beyond,
and into the realm of the expected.

00:09:19.421 --> 00:09:20.261
Thank you.

00:09:20.261 --> 00:09:25.645
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:25.645 --> 00:09:30.109
[MUSIC]

